## 2.3 Gas selectivity
Given the considerable differences in \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) values and gas uptake between \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and other gases, the IAST method was employed to investigate \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) separation ability of \(\mathrm{In-L_{6}}\) -IPA (Figs. S13–17 in the ESM). Figures 3(a) and 3(b) reveal that the equimolar \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6},\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity of \(\mathrm{In-L_{6}}\) -IPA are 3.9/2.9, 5.1/2.8, and \(4.2/4.3\) under \(100\;\mathrm{\kPa}\) and \(273/298\;\mathrm{~K},\) respectively. Although the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity for \(\mathrm{In-L_{6}}\) -IPA is lower than the recorded MOFs with the molecular sieve effect, such as CPL-1-NH2 (119) [42], and UTSA-300a (743) [10], it is superior or comparable to those of leading \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation materials like JXNU-12(F) (4.1) [43], UPC-200(M)-R-L series (1.89-2.25) [44], FJU-6-TATB (3.1) [45], SNNU-26-Mn (2.0) [46], \(\mathrm{CuZn}_{3}(\mathrm{PDDA})_{3}(\mathrm{OH})\) (3.3) [47], NUM-11a (3.0) [48], and \([\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{tzba})_{0.5}(\mathrm{F})(\mathrm{bpy})]\) (2.2) [49]. The selectivity values for equimolar \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) mixtures are calculated to be 126 and 50 at 273 and \(298\;\mathrm{~K},\) respectively, which is better than the reported MOFs including Ni(dpip) (16.6) [50], \(\mathrm{Cu}_{0.5}(\mathrm{tztp})_{0.5}\) (23.0) [51], and SNNU-5-Sc (13.5) [52].
efficient separation of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mixtures achieved by In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA around room temperature, in which \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was first passed through the packed column while \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) retained for longer time. Specifically, the breakthrough time intervals for the equimolar \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mixtures are 29.2 and \(56.2\ \mathrm{min\cdot g^{-1}}\) at 298 and \(273\ \mathrm{K},\) respectively. During this process, the absorbed \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) amount was calculated to be 16.5 and \(31.1~\mathrm{cm\cdot g^{-1}}\) , nearly \(65.5\%\) and \(62.9\%\) of the saturated uptake (25.2 and \(49.4~\mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at 298 and \(273\;\mathrm{~K~}\) under \(5\;\;\mathrm{kPa})\) from the single-component adsorption isotherms. However, the co-adsorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was only 5.3 and \(8.5~\mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) . Thus, the separation factor for equimolar \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) mixtures based on the breakthrough curves ranges up to 3.1 of \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) and 3.6 of \(273\;\mathrm{K},\) which is exceeding or comparable to those of representative materials, such as ZJU-280a (2.1) [7], NKMOF-1- Ni (2.6) [40], CAU-10-H (3.4) [2], SIFSIX-Cu-TPA (1.97) [58], and FJU-22a (1.9) [59]. Furthermore, when the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)
mixtures ratios were adjusted to 1:5, 1:10, and 1: 20, In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA still exhibited a great separation ability.
In addition to efficient \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation, highly efficient separation for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) mixtures can be simultaneously achieved by In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA (Figs. 4(d) and 4(e)). \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) eluted through the adsorption bed to directly yield an outflow of pure \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\) whereas \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was retained in the bed for longer time (with an undetectable \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) signal). The pure \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) production from the outlet of 5/5, 1/5, 1/10, and 1/20 (v/v) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) mixtures were recorded to be 7.1/14.9, 6.5/18.6, 20.3/30.0, and \(26.5/58.1\;\mathrm{L\cdot kg^{-1}}\) at 298/273 K, respectively. The \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) production of In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA is inferior to those of benchmark MOFs such as TIFSIX-2-Ni-I and SIFSIX-2-Cu-I [29], but superior to the values of NbU-1 [60] and HUST-6 [61]. It can be found that the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) production increases with the increase of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) content in the mixtures. Thus, In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA is hopeful to yield high purity \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) from the industrially common
\(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) (1/99, v/v) mixtures. Moreover, for the ratios of 5/5, 1/5, 1/10, and 1/20 (v/v) \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) mixtures, the calculated separation factor in the range of 1.7–2.5 at two temperatures agrees well with the result by IAST selectivity. In general, the \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) mixtures include \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) and \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) impurities, so the breakthrough curves of equimolar \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{6}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}\) mixtures were also studied. As depicted in Figs. S18 and S19 in the ESM, In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA can efficiently separate these mixtures, wherein \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) or \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) first broke through the adsorption bed while \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) eluted through occurred at 46/85 and \(42/70\ \mathrm{min\cdotg^{-1}}\) at \(298/273\mathrm{~K~}\) for equimolar \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C H_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) mixtures, respectively.
To ensure the recyclability and stability of In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA, equimolar \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}\) separation cycling experiments with Ar flow regeneration at \(323\mathrm{~K~}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) were carried out at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) (Figs. 4(c) and 4(f)). The results demonstrated that there is no obvious decrease in separation performance and breakthrough time within five continuous cycles. Furthermore, the structural stability of In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA was verified by PXRD patterns of collected after breakthrough experiments (Fig. S4 in the ESM).
Besides, In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA can also separate \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) from more complex equimolar ternary acetylene-containing mixtures \((\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{4}/}\) \(\mathrm{CH_{4}},\,\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{6}/C O_{2}},\,\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}/C O_{2}},\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}})\) by a single breakthrough operation. As divulged in Fig. 5 and Fig. S20 in the ESM, \(\mathrm{CH_{4}},\,\mathrm{CO_{2}},\,\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}},\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) flow out from the outlet firstly after the gas mixtures enter the packed bed, while the retention time of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) is the longest. The breakthrough time of various gases in packed bed of In- \(\mathrm{L}_{6}\) -IPA is consistent with the uptake of single component and \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) value. For instance, for the \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}/C O_{2}}\) mixtures, \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) flow out from the outlet successively after introducing the gas mixture into the bed, while \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) can be retained in the bed as long as \(38.5\,\mathrm{\min{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) . The captured amounts of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) from these ternary mixtures are in the range of \(14.4–16.9\ \mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) based on the multinary breakthrough curves.
## 2.5 GCMC simulations
GCMC simulations were carried out at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) under \(100\ \mathrm{kPa}\) to investigate the host–guest interactions. As depicted in Figs. 6(a)–6(c), four main locations of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) molecules near the ligands in framework were distinguished. \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) -I and -II exist in similar
locations near \(\mathrm{IPA}^{2-}\) and \(\mathrm{L}_{6}^{\mathrm{~6-~}}\) linkers of small channels through \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots O/\pi}\) interactions (H···O, 2.978–3.348 Å; H···π, 3.221 Å) with O atoms of carboxyl groups and the phenyl –CH lateral groups as well as \(\mathrm{C-H_{C_{2}H_{4}}\cdots T C_{2}H_{2}}\) interaction between \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) -I and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) -II molecules (Fig. 6(a)). \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) -III is located near the \(\mathrm{L}_{6}^{\mathrm{~6-~}}\) linkers and is fixed by the two \(\mathrm{O}\!\cdot\!\pi\) and three \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots\pi}\) interactions generated between the carboxylate O atoms, phenyl –CH groups, and alkynyl \(\pi\) cloud of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) (Fig. 6(b)). \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) -IV was involved in two \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots\pi}\) and \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots O}\) contacts with the carboxylate O atoms, –CH group, and \(\pi\) center of phenyl in large channels (Fig. 6(c)).
In contrast, there are three main adsorption sites for \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) and two important sites for \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\) interacting with the framework. As given in Figs. 6(d) and 6(e), \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) -I and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) -II are located in the similar ring-like structure composed of \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\), \(\mathrm{IPA}^{2-}\), and partially of \(\mathrm{L}_{6}^{\mathrm{~6-~}}\) linkers to form one \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots\pi}\) interaction and multiple \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots O}\) hydrogen bonds with the phenyl and O atoms of carbonyl group in large channels. In addition, similar \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots O/\pi}\) contacts were also observed between \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\) -III and the phenyl and carboxylate O atoms in \(\mathrm{L}_{6}^{\mathrm{~6-~}}\) linkers (Fig. 6(f)). For \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\), \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) -I and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) -II molecules are resided in the region of carbonyl groups between two \(\mathrm{L}_{6}^{\mathrm{~6-~}}\) linkers of large channels, in which form both \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots\pi/O}\) interactions with the carboxylate O atoms, \(\pi\) center of phenyl (Fig. 6(g)). \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) -III forms the similar \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots\pi/O}\) interactions with both \(\mathrm{IPA}^{2-}\) and \(\mathrm{O}\,\,6^{-}\) linker in small channels (Fig. 6(h)). For \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\) molecular, the O atoms in both \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\) - I and \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\) -II interact with the –CH group of phenyl through strong \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots O}\) hydrogen bonds with the H···O distances in the range of 2.618 and \(3.365\,\mathrm{\AA}\) . Meanwhile, the C atoms of \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\) -I and \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\) -II interact with carboxylate O atoms through C···O interactions \((3.107{-}3.309\mathrm{~\AA~})\) . In short, besides more and stronger \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots\pi/O}\) interactions, the additional \(\mathrm{C-H_{C2H2}\cdots T C_{2}H_{2}}\) interaction between two \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) molecules and \(\mathrm{O}\!\cdot\!\pi\) contacts was also formed for \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) compared to \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\), \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\), and \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\) molecules. These crucial factors are responsible for the higher \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) uptake and outstanding selectivity for \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) over \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\), \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}\), and \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\).
## 2.6 Stability test
Given that the real-industrial of \(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}\) -relevant separation tasks are commonly performed under water vapor or humidity conditions, which requires adsorbents with long-term and high water and
humidity stabilities, we thus examined the stability of In- \( \mathrm{L}_{6} \)-IPA toward air environments, relative humidity, and water for a long time, monitored by gas adsorption experiments and PXRD tests. As shown in Fig. 7(a), after exposing to water for 1 day, \( 65\% \) humidity for 10 days, and air for 30 days, In- \( \mathrm{L}_{6} \)-IPA remains intact without loss of crystallinity and phase transformation, as verified by PXRD. Moreover, \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) adsorption isotherms after same treatment are close to those of desolvated samples, further supporting the high chemical stability of In- \( \mathrm{L}_{6} \)-IPA. The excellent stability on one hand might be attributed to the formation of strong In–O coordination bonds, on the other hand, the \( \mathrm{In}^{3+} \) ion exhibits a \( [\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}] \) eight coordinated geometry surrounded without open metal sites, and \( \mathrm{In}^{3+} \) center is shielded by phenyl groups, which prevent the attack on the metal center from water.
## 3 Conclusions
In a nutshell, we have investigated the utilization of a novel hexacarboxylate organic linker [1,1;3;1"-terphenyl]-3,3",4',5,5",6'-hexacarboxylic acid and \( \mathrm{In}^{3+} \) ions to construct a robust 3D indiumorganic framework (In- \( \mathrm{L}_{6} \)-IPA), featuring two types of 1D honeycomb channels for the separation of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \). With the advantages of excellent stability, high adsorption capacity for \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) (104.4 cm3·g-1, the second highest value among all reported indium-based MOFs), selective adsorption behavior for \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) over \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \), \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6} \), and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \), outstanding regeneration and recycle properties.
In- \( \mathrm{L}_{6} \)-IPA was considered to be a promising candidate for critical \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) separation applications. Importantly, In- \( \mathrm{L}_{6} \)-IPA can efficiently capture and separate \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) from binary \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4} \) mixtures as well as challenging ternary \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6} \) mixtures, as revealed by dynamic breakthrough experiments. These great separation performances result from different binding interactions strength of the framework with \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) and other gases, as evidenced by the GCMC calculation. This work not only reports a robust indiumorganic framework for efficient separation of \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \) from \( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2} \)-containing mixtures, but also broadens the diversity of indium-based MOFs for targeting challenging multicomponent separation.
